The fixed heap address of ghc-bootstrap's rts/patches/0003-mblock-map-window.patch
for aarch64 and riscv64 as well.  Without it the heap comes from an unhinted
mmap, outside the window the mblock map covers, and getMBlocks fails at once.

The window, 0x500000000 to 0x1500000000, lies between the C heap and the mmap
area on both.  A non-PIE aarch64 Linux process has the x86-64 layout: the
executable at 0x400000, the C heap after it and the mmap area at the top of
the address space.  A non-PIE riscv64 process has its executable at 0x10000,
and with the 39-bit virtual addresses of most current hardware, 256 GB of
address space.  Its mmap area starts near the top, or, with an unlimited stack
as in the chain's builds, at a third of it (0x1555556000), still above the
window.

--- a/ghc/rts/MBlock.h
+++ b/ghc/rts/MBlock.h
@@ -23,7 +23,8 @@
 #error I have no idea where to begin the heap on a non-64-bit osf3 machine.
 #endif
 
-#elif defined(x86_64_TARGET_ARCH) && SIZEOF_VOID_P == 8
+#elif (defined(x86_64_TARGET_ARCH) || defined(aarch64_TARGET_ARCH) \
+       || defined(riscv64_TARGET_ARCH)) && SIZEOF_VOID_P == 8
 /* Ask for the heap from a fixed address, as 6.4 does on x86-64: above the
  * executable and the C heap (a non-PIE binary sits at 0x400000 and brk
  * grows from its end) and below the mmap area (0x7f...).  With HEAP_BASE 0
